<SectionLayout
  slug="supply-chain"
  kicker="Section 05"
  title="Supply chain"
  dek="From chip design to a finished GPU — TSMC, ASML, HBM makers, and why export controls exist."
>
  <PlainEnglish>
    <p>
      NVIDIA designs GPUs but doesn't make them — almost every advanced AI chip in the world is
      physically manufactured by one company in Taiwan (TSMC), using machines that only one
      company in the world can build (ASML, in the Netherlands). That makes the entire AI industry
      dependent on a tiny handful of companies and a couple of buildings, and it's why the U.S.
      government restricts which countries are allowed to buy the most advanced chips.
    </p>
  </PlainEnglish>

  <h2>The chain, from raw silicon to a working GPU</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Chip design</strong> — NVIDIA, AMD, Google, etc. design the chip's architecture, but own no factories themselves ("fabless").</li>
    <li><strong>Fabrication</strong> — the design gets physically manufactured onto silicon wafers, almost entirely by <strong>TSMC</strong> (Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company), which produces the overwhelming majority of the world's most advanced chips, including essentially all of NVIDIA's GPUs.</li>
    <li><strong>Lithography equipment</strong> — TSMC's most advanced manufacturing steps require extreme ultraviolet (EUV) lithography machines, which only <strong>ASML</strong> (a Dutch company) makes. Without ASML's machines, TSMC's cutting-edge fabrication doesn't exist — this single-supplier chokepoint is one of the most important facts in the entire industry.</li>
    <li><strong>Advanced packaging</strong> — the manufactured chip has to be bonded together with its HBM memory using TSMC's advanced packaging processes (called CoWoS); this step has itself become a bottleneck, with NVIDIA reportedly controlling around half of the available advanced-packaging capacity industry-wide.</li>
    <li><strong>Memory (HBM)</strong> — the high-bandwidth memory stacked onto the chip is made by a small group of memory manufacturers — primarily <strong>SK Hynix</strong>, <strong>Samsung</strong>, and <strong>Micron</strong> — and HBM shortages/price increases have been a recurring bottleneck alongside packaging capacity.</li>
  </ol>
  <SupplyChainDiagram />

  <div class="callout-note">
    <strong>Wait, who's ASML?</strong> Most readers have never heard of ASML, despite it arguably being the
    single most important company in the entire AI chip industry. It is the <em>only</em> company on Earth that
    makes the extreme ultraviolet (EUV) lithography machines needed to print the finest details on the most
    advanced chips — without them, none of TSMC's cutting-edge fabrication happens at all.
  </div>

  <h2>Why this is a chokepoint, not just a supply chain</h2>
  <p>
    Nearly the entire advanced-AI-chip industry runs through: one company's fabs (TSMC), one company's
    lithography machines (ASML), and three memory makers. That concentration is exactly why export controls work
    as a policy tool — restricting a handful of companies' ability to sell into a country meaningfully restricts
    that country's access to frontier AI chips, and it's why the U.S. has coordinated with Japan and the
    Netherlands (ASML's home country) to align export rules rather than acting alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Export controls, in brief</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The U.S. restricts the sale of the most advanced AI chips (and the equipment used to make them) to China, on national-security grounds.</li>
    <li>As of 2026, chipmakers with China operations (TSMC, Samsung, SK Hynix) must apply for annual export licenses rather than operating under blanket exemptions — a policy that can change terms every year.</li>
    <li>Analysts estimate the U.S. holds something like a 20-50x advantage in AI compute produced in 2026 versus China, though estimates vary and China is pursuing its own domestic chip industry in response — the intent of the controls is less to fully block China's AI progress and more to keep it meaningfully more expensive and less efficient.</li>
    <li>Enforcement is imperfect: there have been documented cases of restricted chips reaching China through intermediary countries or corporate structures, prompting new rules in places like Malaysia requiring advance notice of any chip re-export.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="policy-note">
    This is a live, contested policy area — presented here as mechanics and stated rationale, not a verdict on
    whether the controls are good policy.
  </p>
</SectionLayout>

